### ✅ Existing API Endpoints (EC2 Port 8000)
```
POST /session/start     → returns { session_id, wg_config, status }
POST /session/end/{id}  → tears down session
POST /events/{id}       → receives client telemetry events
GET  /report/{id}       → returns session report
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Do NOT modify the backend unless something is clearly broken. The entire intelligence pipeline is stable. Focus is 100% on the Desktop Client.

---

## What Needs To Be Built: Desktop Client MVP

### Goal
A minimal Electron desktop app that:
1. Shows a **Session Login Screen** (candidate enters session code)
2. **Calls** `POST /session/start` on the EC2 backend
3. **Writes** the returned `wg_config` to a temp file
4. **Activates** WireGuard via the OS (`wg-quick up`)
5. Shows **Connection Status** (tunnel up / backend reachable / monitoring active)
6. On exit → calls `POST /session/end/{id}` and tears down the tunnel

---

## Desktop Client File Structure

```
desktop_client/
├── electron_app/
│   ├── main.js           ← Electron main process (window, IPC, wg-quick calls)
│   ├── preload.js        ← Secure IPC bridge to renderer
│   ├── package.json
│   ├── renderer/
│   │   ├── index.html    ← Login screen UI
│   │   ├── status.html   ← Connection status UI
│   │   └── app.js        ← Renderer logic (calls preload IPC)
│   └── assets/
│       └── logo.png
└── python_bridge/
    ├── session_client.py     ← Calls EC2 FastAPI (start/end session)
    └── tunnel_manager.py     ← Writes wg config, runs wg-quick
```

> [!NOTE]
> `python_bridge/` is called by Electron via `child_process.spawn()`. This keeps Node.js and Python cleanly separated. No npm packages needed for tunnel logic.

