## Data Flow — Desktop Client ↔ EC2

```
[User enters session code]
         ↓
[Renderer] → IPC "start-session" → [main.js]
         ↓
[main.js] spawns → [session_client.py]
         ↓
POST http://3.235.42.166:8000/session/start
         ↓
EC2 returns: { session_id, wg_config }
         ↓
[tunnel_manager.py] writes /tmp/coadex-<id>.conf
         ↓
`wg-quick up /tmp/coadex-<id>.conf` (requires sudo/admin)
         ↓
ALL system traffic now routes through EC2
         ↓
EC2 monitor_v3.py captures DNS queries from candidate IP
```

---

## Critical Implementation Notes

### WireGuard Activation
- **Windows**: Use `wireguard.exe /installtunnelservice <config_path>` (needs admin)
- **Linux/Mac**: `wg-quick up <config_path>` (needs sudo)
- The Electron app must request elevated privileges on launch.

### Security
- Never store `client_privkey` to disk after tunnel is active — keep in memory only.
- Write WireGuard config to `/tmp/` (Linux) or `%TEMP%` (Windows) and delete after `wg-quick up`.

### What the EC2 Backend Needs (Already Done)
The `/session/start` endpoint already:
- Generates a WireGuard key pair.
- Returns a valid client `wg_config`.
- The only thing missing is: **it must also call `wg set wg0 peer <client_pubkey> allowed-ips <client_ip>/32`** on the EC2 side to register the client. This needs to be wired up properly in [main.py](file:///c:/Users/mohap/OneDrive/Desktop/Coadex%202_0/ec2_backend/main.py).

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Backend fix needed before Desktop Client will work**: `POST /session/start` must actually add the client as a WireGuard peer via `WireGuardTunnel.add_peer()`. Currently it generates the config but doesn't register the peer on `wg0`. Without this, the tunnel handshake will fail.

